\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Support parity, group degree and sign sectors}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{For the four-cycle over a nonzero commutative ring with invertible
two, three decompositions record different structures. The
[support grading](fdip-001I) separates fields supported on
#{\{0,2\}} and #{\{1,3\}}. The one-step shift
#{Tf(i)=f(i+1)} exchanges these supports, so it is odd, whereas
#{P=T^2} and #{E_\pm=h(I\pm P)} preserve them and are even.
A single [half-turn sign sector](fdip-000L) contains both
#{b_{\varepsilon,0}=\delta_0+\varepsilon\delta_2} and
#{b_{\varepsilon,1}=\delta_1+\varepsilon\delta_3}, which have
different support parities. A minus eigenvalue does not mean odd support.}

\p{The [canonical group grading](fgap-001U) of #{k[C_2]} has
components #{k\,1} of degree #{1_G} and #{k\,g} of degree #{r}.
Their multiplication follows the group law, in particular #{g^2=1}.
Over a ring these are rank-one free summands. The representation sends
#{g} to support-even #{P}; it therefore does not preserve these
particular gradings under #{r\mapsto1\in\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z}.
The projector ideals are different again: #{e_-^2=e_-\ne0}, while
an odd homogeneous component would have its square in the even component.
The direct product, the group grading and the support grading cannot be
interchanged. These are the [distinct splitting levels](fgap-0012)
applied to the concrete action.}

\p{The ordinary adjoint uses the [counting pairing](fdip-001F)
#{b(f,u)=\sum_i f_i u_i}, with identity coefficient involution.
Delta vectors recover all coefficients, so the pairing is perfect.
Reindexing a finite sum shows that every permutation action is an
isometry, hence
##{b(\rho(\sigma)f,u)=b(f,\rho(\sigma^{-1})u).}
Uniqueness of the [ordinary adjoint](fdip-001H) gives
#{\rho(\sigma)^\dagger=\rho(\sigma^{-1})}; in particular
#{T^\dagger=T^{-1}} and #{P^\dagger=P}. There is no complex
conjugation or Koszul sign in this calculation.}

\p{The corresponding ordinary group-algebra operation is
##{\left(\sum_g a_g[g]\right)^*=\sum_g a_g[g^{-1}].}
It is #{k}-linear, fixes scalars, squares to the identity and reverses
products, since #{(gh)^{-1}=h^{-1}g^{-1}} and all sums are finite.
For an isometric representation with a perfect pairing, taking the
adjoint of its finite linear extension therefore gives
#{\widehat\rho(z^*)=\widehat\rho(z)^\dagger}.
The isometry premise matters: the infinite cyclic group acting on
#{\mathbb R} by multiplication by #{2} has adjoint #{2} and inverse
#{1/2}, so this identity fails for that representation. The counting
action satisfies the premise.}

\p{The group basis and inversion are as in
\citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations};
the grading and adjoint comparisons above are direct calculations.
The [shift calculation](fdip-001M) supplies the support-degree and
adjoint identities. Clifford parity of a quadratic quotient is a further
structure; these ordinary formulas introduce neither a graded star nor
a Hermitian extension.}
